 ## Subtopics
 
-### [Fresnel Method](52-fresnel-method.md)
-An approximation of wave propagation that is computationally less intensive than ASM but relies on the paraxial approximation. Choose ASM when high accuracy or non-paraxial propagation is required.
-
-### [Rayleigh-Sommerfeld Diffraction](53-rayleigh-sommerfeld.md)
-Another exact propagation method based on Green's functions, often more expensive than ASM due to convolution requirements. ASM is preferred for its efficiency via FFT.
+### [Camera-Calibrated Differentiable Holographic Model](17687-camera-calibrated-differentiable-holographic-model.md)
+A differentiable computational model that simulates the physical propagation of light from a Spatial Light Modulator (SLM) to a camera sensor, incorporating learnable parameters to account for system-specific imperfections such as polarization, SLM curvature, beam profiles, higher diffraction orders, crosstalk, and Lookup Table (LUT) non-linearity. It sets itself apart from standard Angular Spectrum Method (ASM) implementations by explicitly modeling the superposition of zero and first-order diffraction patterns through a neural network (U-Net) and calibrating these parameters against experimental data, thereby compensating for unmodeled physical effects in compact optical setups.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N^2 \log N)$ due to the two 2D Fast Fourier Transforms (FFTs) [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^2)$ to store the input field, frequency grid, and transfer function [S1].
-- **Performance:** More computationally intensive than the Fresnel Method because it requires two full Fourier Transforms rather than a single convolution or multiplication in the spatial domain [S1].
-- **Strengths:** No approximations; exact for free-space propagation; handles large angles and near-field propagation effectively [S1].
-- **Limitations:** High computational cost; sensitive to aliasing if the sampling rate is insufficient (Nyquist criterion must be met for the highest spatial frequencies) [S1].
-- **When to use:** High-resolution hologram reconstruction, non-paraxial scenarios, or when exact phase information is critical [S1].
-- **When not to use:** Real-time applications where speed is prioritized over exactness, or when paraxial approximations are valid (Fresnel Method is faster) [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** Requires two 2D Fourier Transforms. Complexity is $O(MN \log(MN))$ for an $M \times N$ array, similar to Fresnel propagation, but with a higher constant factor due to the exact phase calculation [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the input field, the angular spectrum, the transfer function, and the output field. $O(MN)$ complex numbers.
+- **Accuracy:** Exact solution to the free-space wave equation; no paraxial or Fresnel approximations [S1].
+- **Strengths:** High accuracy for near-field and wide-angle propagation; handles evanescent waves (if implemented).
+- **Limitations:** Computationally more expensive than Fresnel method; sensitive to sampling conditions; requires handling of evanescent waves [S1].
+- **When to use:** When high accuracy is required, especially for near-field propagation or when the paraxial approximation is invalid.
+- **When not to use:** When computational efficiency is paramount and the Fresnel approximation is valid.
 
 ## Background
-The Angular Spectrum Method was introduced as an alternative reconstruction method for digital holography that avoids the approximations inherent in the Fresnel Method [S1]. It is based on the principle that any optical field can be decomposed into a sum of plane waves, each propagating in a different direction [S1]. The method has been widely adopted in computer-generated holography and optical reconstruction due to its accuracy [S1, S2]. Recent advances have explored software implementations to improve computational efficiency, especially for high-resolution and color holograms [S1]. It is also used in mesh-represented and learning-empowered hologram synthesis as a fundamental propagation operator [S2].
+The Angular Spectrum Method is a fundamental technique in wave optics and digital holography. It is based on the principle that any wavefield can be decomposed into a sum of plane waves (the angular spectrum) [S1]. The method was developed as an exact alternative to approximate propagation methods like the Fresnel and Fraunhofer approximations. It is widely used in computer-generated holography (CGH) and digital holographic reconstruction [S1, S2]. The method is referenced in recent advances in processing and rendering algorithms for CGH [S1] and in mesh-represented hologram synthesis [S2].
 
